-- SHADOW-SEMANTICS cell I18: the interface's home module is imported under a MODULE
-- ALIAS, and that does NOT make the interface nameable here. `import smod as S` cannot put
-- a TYPE name in scope at all -- an alias-qualified name in type position is a parse error
-- -- and writing `impl Sizeable Blob` in this module is rejected `Unknown interface:
-- Sizeable`. That is the SAME evidence i15 cites to conclude "not a shadow", so the two
-- cells must agree: `size` is not a shadow here and the occurrence is the standalone this
-- module explicitly imported from `fmod`: 99.
--
-- ⚠️ PAIRED WITH `named.mdk`, graded as its own row: the same graph with the alias replaced
-- by `import smod.{Sizeable, sf}`, which DOES name the interface. That one prints 7. The
-- pair is the point -- the predicate must answer the two spellings differently, and one
-- number without the other cannot show that it does.
--
-- WHY THIS CELL EXISTS. An earlier cut of the #1353 fix made a module alias admit the
-- aliased module's WHOLE interface row, because doing otherwise regressed #1277 (a closed
-- S0) from (1, 2) to (1, 1). That bought #1277 at the price of this cell: one predicate
-- answering "nameable?" YES for an alias while `impl Sizeable Blob` in the same module
-- answered NO -- 7 vs 99, exit 0, no diagnostic, which is #1353's own symptom one spelling
-- over. The fix is that these are TWO questions: what a module can NAME (this cell, which
-- decides shadow-hood) and what it can CALL (#1277's `A.ff`, which decides marking). See
-- `callableIfaceMethodSet` for why an alias answers them differently.
import fmod.{size}
import smod as S

main = println (size "abc")
